/**
   @page return_main reTurn: STUN/TURN Server and Client Library

   reTurn is a STUN server (RFC 5389, with RFC 3489 compatibility for binding
   requests), a TURN relay server (RFC 5766), and a client library for both.
   It is built on asio and @ref rutil_main "rutil", and needs OpenSSL for
   message integrity and TLS.

   @section return_client Client Library
      The client library lives in reTurn/client, in the reTurn namespace.
      - reTurn::TurnAsyncSocket : The asynchronous client, with
      reTurn::TurnAsyncUdpSocket, reTurn::TurnAsyncTcpSocket and
      reTurn::TurnAsyncTlsSocket for each transport. Its functions, such as
      bindRequest(), createAllocation(), refreshAllocation(),
      setActiveDestination() and send(), return at once; results and
      received data are delivered to a reTurn::TurnAsyncSocketHandler. It
      runs on an asio::io_context supplied by the application, and is what
      @ref reflow_main "reflow" uses.
      - reTurn::TurnSocket : A simpler, blocking client, with
      reTurn::TurnUdpSocket, reTurn::TurnTcpSocket and reTurn::TurnTlsSocket.

      A typical TURN client sets credentials with setUsernameAndPassword(),
      calls createAllocation(), adds its peer with setActiveDestination(),
      and then exchanges data with send() and the handler's onReceiveSuccess()
      callback, calling refreshAllocation() before the allocation expires.

   @section return_server Server
      The reTurnServer application (reTurn/reTurnServer.cxx) listens for STUN
      and TURN over UDP, TCP and TLS, and relays allocations over UDP, for
      IPv4 and IPv6 (RFC 6156). It is configured with reTurnServer.config
      (see reTurn::ReTurnConfig), and authenticates TURN users with the
      long-term credential mechanism against a user database file, which can
      hold plain or hashed passwords.

      The main server classes are reTurn::TurnManager, which allocates relay
      ports and enforces the per-user allocation and per-address connection
      limits; reTurn::TurnAllocationManager, which tracks allocations;
      reTurn::RequestHandler, which processes STUN and TURN requests;
      reTurn::TurnAllocation, a single relayed allocation and its
      permissions and channels; and reTurn::UdpServer, reTurn::TcpServer
      and reTurn::TlsServer, the listening sockets.

      reTurn::StunMessage encodes and decodes STUN and TURN messages, and is
      shared by the client and the server.
*/
